# miated — task prompts

All work happens in `index.html` (single-file PWA; the Rust binary just serves
it). Key symbols: `onFix()` records `route[]` (in-memory `{lat, lon}` array),
`drawRouteBox()` renders the compass dial + route dots, `updateHeading()`
fuses GPS course + magnetometer into `heading`, pinch handlers manage
`userZoom`/`zoomFactor`/`viewScale`. After each task: `cargo check` must pass
(no Rust changes expected) and a manual smoke test in the browser.

---

## Task 1 — Rotate route dots with the compass (heading-up view)

**Problem.** The compass rose rotates so the current heading sits at the top
(`drawCardinals(hdg)`, minor ticks at `i * 10 - hdg`), but the route dots are
drawn north-up: `x = cx + east(p) * s`, `y = cy - north(p) * s`. When the
compass rotates, the dots stay fixed to north — they feel disconnected from
the dial.

**Prompt.** In `drawRouteBox()` (`index.html`), rotate the route-dot layer by
the same heading the rose uses, so the whole radar is heading-up:

- Rotate each dot's local east/north offset by `-hdg` degrees before scaling:
  `x = cx + (east * cos(hdgRad) + north * sin(hdgRad)) * s`,
  `y = cy - (-east * sin(hdgRad) + north * cos(hdgRad)) * s` (derive the exact
  signs so a point that is currently ahead of me always lands "up" on screen;
  verify against a due-north and due-east walk).
- Use the same smoothed `heading` value the rose uses (`hdg = heading || 0`)
  so dots and rose can never be out of phase — one source of truth.
- The current-position dot stays at the center; only the route rotates around
  it. Keep the existing clip to the rounded square.
- No visible jitter: since `heading` is already smoothed in `updateHeading()`,
  no extra easing is needed, but confirm dots don't wobble when standing
  still (compass noise) — if they do, quantize or smooth the rotation applied
  to dots separately from the rose.

**Done when:** rotating the phone (or changing `lastCourse` while moving)
rotates rose and dots together as one rigid scene; a route walked straight
north then south renders as a line pointing down then up respectively.

---

## Task 2 — Persist GPS dots with per-dot creation time; expire after 24 h

**Problem.** `route` is a plain in-memory array (`route.push({lat, lon})` in
`onFix()`); every reload wipes it, and dots live forever within a session.

**Prompt.** Give every dot a creation timestamp and make the route survive
reloads, expiring each dot 24 h after it was created:

- Change the record shape to `{ lat, lon, t }`, setting `t: Date.now()` on
  push in `onFix()` (keep the existing 2 m haversine decimation and the
  1500-point halving, which must preserve `t`).
- Persist to `localStorage` (single key, e.g. `miatedRoute`), debounced —
  write on visibilitychange/pagehide and at most every few seconds on fix,
  not on every GPS update. Wrap in try/catch (private mode / quota).
- On boot, load and prune: drop any dot whose `t` is older than 24 h before
  rendering or storing. Also prune opportunistically on each fix (cheap
  filter when the oldest dot expires) so the 24 h rule holds during long
  sessions. `routeShown`/`routeCard` should still only appear on the first
  GPS fix, but the loaded history must render immediately on that first fix.
- Draw in `drawRouteBox()` must not assume ordering by index for fading —
  see Task 3; for now keep the existing index-based fade working with the
  new shape.

**Done when:** record a route, reload the page, grant GPS again — the old
dots reappear; a dot injected with `t` 25 h in the past never renders and is
removed from storage.

---

## Task 3 — Connect dots with a translucent route line (Apple Watch style)

**Problem.** Dots are drawn as isolated circles with an index-based fade
(`0.3 + age * 0.7`); there is no line linking them into a path.

**Prompt.** In `drawRouteBox()`, connect consecutive route dots with a
slightly translucent polyline, oldest → newest, like the Apple Watch compass
app's route:

- Draw one path through the (already heading-rotated, Task 1) dot positions
  in chronological order: `strokeStyle` a translucent route blue (e.g.
  `rgba(140, 170, 238, 0.45)`), `lineWidth` ≈ `dotR * 1.2`, round
  joins/caps, drawn *under* the dots (draw line first, then dots, then the
  green current-position marker).
- Ends at the current position: extend the line from the newest dot to the
  center point so the path visually reaches "me".
- Fade along the route: either a low-alpha base stroke plus per-segment
  alpha that ramps 0.15 → 0.6 from oldest to newest (batched, no per-segment
  stroke if it hurts frame rate), or a single gradient stroke if measurably
  cheaper. Keep it subtle — dots remain the primary visual.
- Must compose with Task 2's timestamps: order/fade should derive from `t`
  (chronological), not array index, once Task 2 lands.

**Done when:** a walked loop renders as a connected translucent loop through
every dot, newest end touching the green marker, no line segments crossing
the dial's rounded border (clip already handles this — verify corners).

---

## Task 4 — Auto-zoom that fits all dots exactly; snappier manual zoom

**Problem.** Auto zoom currently fits `maxD` (max dot distance, min 25 m)
into `dialHalf * 0.72` and eases with `dt * 3`; manual pinch overrides for
5 s then eases back. The fit can zoom out far more than needed (min 25 m is
large when standing still) and manual zoom feels laggy (slow easing, 5 s
timeout).

**Prompt.** Two related tuning changes in `drawRouteBox()` and the pinch
handlers:

- **Auto-fit, minimal zoom-out:** compute the bounding box of all live dots
  *in rotated screen space* (after Task 1's rotation), then choose scale to
  fit that box into the dial with a small margin — not the max radial
  distance into a fixed 0.72 factor. Never zoom out more than necessary:
  the scale must be `min(fitAll, currentScaleNeededForNewestDotOnly)` style
  monotone growth — zooming out only when a new dot actually exceeds the
  fit, and gently zooming back in when dots expire (Task 2) or the route
  shrinks. Lower the floor from 25 m to something like 5 m so a stationary
  user isn't pushed way out.
- **Snappier manual zoom:** raise the manual easing constant (`dt * 3` →
  `dt * 12` or similar for `zoomFactor` while pinching), apply pinch
  scaling to the rendered scale immediately (update `viewScale` target from
  `userZoom` without double smoothing), extend the clamp sensibly, and
  lengthen the manual-override window (5 s → ~10 s) so it doesn't snap back
  mid-inspection; the ease-*back* to auto can stay slow and gentle.

**Done when:** walking away zooms out in tight steps that just keep the
farthest dot inside the dial; pinching responds within a frame or two and
holds for ~10 s before drifting back to auto-fit.

---

## Task 5 — Share the route view (double-tap the GPS card)

**Problem.** No way to export the route. `fitny` and `mood-meter` (sibling
projects in this workspace) already have the exact pattern to copy.

**Prompt.** Add a share feature to miated's route card, reusing the share
pattern from `fitny/index.html` (search `canShareFiles`, `daySummaryBlob`,
`navigator.share` — feature-detect `navigator.canShare({files})`, share a
rendered PNG via the system sheet, fall back to a plain download on failure
or unsupported platforms; AbortError = dismissed, do nothing) and
`mood-meter/index.html` (download/copy/share button row):

- **Trigger:** double-tap (two taps < 350 ms apart, using Pointer Events on
  `routeCanvas`; must not fight the existing pinch pointers — ignore taps
  that are part of a 2-finger gesture). On trigger, show brief feedback
  (e.g. flash the dial border or a toast) while rendering.
- **Render:** draw the route view as a shareable image — offscreen canvas at
  2× the card size showing the dial, the connected translucent route with
  dots (Tasks 1+3 visuals, heading-up), the green position marker, plus a
  small caption strip with distance traveled (sum the haversine over live
  dots) and the date. Keep it self-contained — refactor the drawing so the
  share render reuses the same projection math rather than duplicating it.
- **Actions:** system share sheet with the PNG (`miated_<date>.png`, title
  "miated — route"); if sharing files is unavailable, copy to clipboard if
  possible, else download. Handle secure-context limitations the same way
  fitny does.

**Done when:** double-tapping the GPS card on a phone opens the OS share
sheet with a PNG of the dotted route; on desktop it downloads/copies the
same image; single taps and pinches still work as before.
